Package: honets
Title: Higher-Order Network Analysis
Version: 0.3.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Mohammed", "Saqr", email = "saqr@saqr.me", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-5881-3109"))
    )
Description: Build and analyze higher-order networks, in which a relation
    can involve more than two nodes or can depend on more than the current
    node. Covers the three structure families of the higher-order network
    literature Battiston et al. (2020) <doi:10.1016/j.physrep.2020.05.004>,
    plus a text family that reads a corpus as a hypergraph.
    Memory networks from categorical sequences: higher-order networks with
    rule extraction Xu, Wickramarathne, and Chawla (2016)
    <doi:10.1126/sciadv.1600028>, higher-order network embedding Saebi et
    al. (2020) <doi:10.1089/big.2019.0169>, hypergeometric path anomaly
    detection LaRock et al. (2020) <doi:10.1137/1.9781611976236.52>, the
    multi-order generative model Scholtes (2017)
    <doi:10.1145/3097983.3098145>, higher-order centralities Scholtes,
    Wider, and Garas (2016) <doi:10.1140/epjb/e2016-60663-0>, a
    permutation-based Markov order test, a path-dependence diagnostic, and
    bootstrap and permutation inference for extracted rules. Simplicial
    complexes: clique, Vietoris-Rips and pathway complexes, Betti numbers,
    persistent homology, persistence landscapes, bottleneck and Wasserstein
    distances,
    and q-analysis. Hypergraphs: construction from network cliques, sliding
    windows over sequences, bipartite group membership, or embedding
    nearest neighbours; structural measures; tensor eigenvector
    centralities Benson (2019) <doi:10.1137/18M1203031>; PageRank with
    edge-dependent vertex weights Chitra and Raphael (2019)
    <doi:10.48550/arXiv.1905.08287>; the weighted normalized hypergraph
    Laplacian of Zhou, Huang, and Scholkopf (2006) with spectral,
    symmetric-NMF, and joint-NMF clustering Hayashi et al. (2020)
    <doi:10.1145/3340531.3412034> and transductive label spreading;
    projections to weighted graphs and s-line graphs; temporal snapshots;
    hyperedge betweenness and closeness; Infomap ensembles with adjusted-
    mutual-information medoid selection; Y/T/O motif profiles and null models
    by degree-preserving swaps and the configuration model; and hypergraph neural
    networks (HGNN, HyperGAT, heterogeneous HGAT, HyperGCN, HNHN,
    AllDeepSets and AllSetTransformer) in native 'torch'. Text hypergraphs: a
    corpus becomes a weighted document-word hypergraph in either
    orientation (bag of words with smoothed tf-idf, token windows, or
    embedding nearest neighbours), analysed with tidy verbs for clustering,
    keyword extraction, few-label classification, and null testing.
